    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-9-17-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/arts/publishing-industry/translation-of-iranian-literature/",
    "name": "Translation of Iranian Literature",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/arts/publishing-industry/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Translation has introduced international readers to Iranian poetry, fiction, and literary criticism through selected works and editions. The English-language record reveals both influential translations and substantial delays between literary innovation in Iran and its reception abroad.",
    "tags": ["Literary translation", "Iranian literature", "Persian literature", "Translators", "International publishing"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:49:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:49:04Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Translation of Iranian Literature: Works, Voices and Reception",
      "description": "Explore how Iranian literature reaches English readers through translators, selected editions, and a history of uneven international reception."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A translated literature is a selection",
        "content": "Translation gives readers access to particular works, rather than a complete or evenly distributed picture of a literary culture. Michael Beard's historical survey in Encyclopaedia Iranica documents long intervals between developments in modern Persian writing and their appearance in English. A translated bookshelf therefore reflects the history of selection and publication as well as the literature being translated. [s1]\n\nPersian literature also extends beyond the borders of Iran. The Library of Congress describes modern Persian writing across Iran, Afghanistan, Tajikistan, and diaspora communities. This page focuses on Iranian literature while recognizing that the Persian-language field is broader. Its researched examples concern English translation; they cannot stand for reception in every other language or for all languages spoken and written in Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "uneven-reception",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Modern writing reached English unevenly",
        "content": "Beard identifies Edward Browne's translations as an important early route into modern Persian writing for English readers. Browne's *Press and Poetry* appeared in 1914 and presented poetry associated with the Constitutional Revolution, the movement for constitutional government in early twentieth-century Iran. His later literary history included satires by Ali-Akbar Dehkhoda, making political and social commentary part of this translated encounter. [s1]\n\nThe same survey describes a lag in the international circulation of later innovations. Fiction by Mohammad-Ali Jamalzadeh, Bozorg Alavi, and Sadeq Hedayat, together with Nima Yushij's departures from traditional verse forms, began reaching Western readers more substantially after the Second World War. Beard highlights the 1957 English translation of Hedayat's *The Blind Owl* by Desmond P. Costello. These examples show why the publication date of a translation should be distinguished from the date and setting of the original work. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "writers-and-editions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read beyond a single emblematic author",
        "content": "The Library of Congress places Hedayat's fiction alongside other forms of modern expression, including Ahmad Shamlou's poetry and translations into Persian. That broader setting matters: literature moves both into and out of Persian, and an Iranian writer's engagement with other literatures can be part of the work's own history. Hedayat's *The Blind Owl* is an important entry point, but it should not be treated as a substitute for the diversity of Iranian writing. [s2]\n\nThe American Institute of Iranian Studies' Lois Roth Persian Translation Prize provides concrete examples of English editions across genres. Its list includes Iraj Pezeshkzad's comic novel *My Uncle Napoleon*, translated by Dick Davis; Simin Behbahani's *A Cup of Sin*, translated by Farzaneh Milani and Kaveh Safa; and Forugh Farrokhzad's *Sin*, translated by Sholeh Wolpé. These examples put translators visibly beside authors and demonstrate that modern fiction and women's poetry belong within the translated field. The prize list documents recognition, not sales or a complete translation bibliography. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-translation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keep the edition in view",
        "content": "For an international reader, the most useful starting questions are concrete: who translated the work, when did that edition appear, and how does its introduction locate the original? The scholarly history and prize bibliography show that these details belong to the story of reception. Reading across several authors, genres, and translators offers a firmer introduction than assuming that one celebrated edition represents Iranian literature as a whole. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
